ort from our major funders, cite41†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL157: cite42†About†info.arxiv.org · cite43†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite44†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite45†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite46†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite49†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L158: \nL159: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the Nielsen realisation problem for cyclic groups of prime order (https://arxiv.org/html/2608.30620v1)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2608.30620v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1330\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract. L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:     1. cite7†1.1 Additional results L18:     2. cite8†1.2 Recent literature on coherent actions L19:     3. cite9†1.3 Conventions L20:     4. cite10†1.4 Acknowledgements L21:   3. cite11†2 Setup and decompositions from equivariant Poincaré duality L22:     1. cite12†2.1 Good decompositions and isovariant Poincaré spaces L23:     2. cite13†2.2 Finiteness L24:     3. cite14†2.3 Existence of finite isovariant structures for $X_{\\mathrm{Bor}}$ L25:   4. cite15†3 Surgery-theoretic preliminaries L26:     1. cite16†3.1 $K$- and $L$-theory L27:     2. cite17†3.2 $L$-theory with transfers L28:     3. cite18†3.3 $L$-theory for pairs of spaces and structure spectra L29:     4. cite19†3.4 Total surgery obstructions L30:   5. cite20†4 Structure spectra of fixed-point complements L31:     1. cite21†4.1 Outline and first observations L32:     2. cite22†4.2 Davis–Lück assembly and the Farrell–Jones conjecture L33:     3. cite23†4.3 $\\mathrm{L}$-theoretic computations L34:     4. cite24†4.4 $\\mathrm{K}$-theory L35:     5. cite25†4.5 The structure spectrum assembly map and injectivity of the transfer L36:   6. cite26†5 Gluing L37:     1. cite27†5.1 Manifold approximate fibrations and teardrop neighborhoods L38:     2. cite28†5.2 $K$-theoretic obstructions to approximate fibering L39:   7. cite29†6 The main theorem L40:     1. cite30†6.1 Manifold structures on the complement L41:     2. cite31†6.2 Putting together L42:   8. cite32†A Group theory L43:   9. cite33†B Pairs of spaces with transfers L44:   10. cite34†References L45: cite35†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L46: \nL47: arXiv:2608.30620v1 [math.GT] 31 Aug 2026\nL48: # On the Nielsen realisation problem for cyclic groups of prime order\nL49: \nL50: Christian KREMER\nL51: \nL52: Date: August 31, 2026\nL53: ###### Abstract.\nL54: We solve the Nielsen realisation problem for high-dimensional aspherical manifolds in a new class of special cases. Mainly, we focus on actions of cyclic groups of prime order. A novelty is that it gives topological solutions to the problem with certain high-dimensional fixed point sets, whereas previous solutions of this type were restricted to discrete fixed point sets.\nL55: This is enabled by recent joint work with Kirstein on isovariant Poincaré duality spaces, and Farrell–Lück–Steimle’s obstruction theory to finding approximate fibrations within a homotopy class.\nL56: ## 1. Introduction\nL57: Nielsen realisation problems in manifold theory are about refining symmetries of manifolds to stronger symmetries. Jakob Nielsen [cite36†Nie32 ] originally asked if a finite subgroup of the mapping class group of a surface can be refined to a group action. For example, if $f$ is a selfmap of a surface $\\Sigma_{g}$ with $f^{p}$ homotopic to the identity, is it homotopic to a homeomorphism of order $p$? There are various ways to generalise Nielsen’s problem to other classes of manifolds $M$.\nL58: This article focuses on a generalisation for aspherical manifolds: closed, connected manifolds with contractible universal cover. Examples of aspherical manifolds are closed Riemannian manifolds of nonpositive sectional curvature, by the celebrated Cartan–Hadamard theorem. To formulate a version of the Nielsen realisation problem, let us write the outer automorphism group of a discrete group $\\pi$ by $\\mathrm{Out}(\\pi)$.\nL59: If $M$ is an aspherical manifold, then $\\mathrm{Out}(\\pi_{1}(M))$ is identified with the group of homotopy classes of homotopy automorphisms of $M$. Hence, subgroups of $\\mathrm{Out}(\\pi_{1}(M))$ encode symmetries of $M$ in a weak sense.\nL60: ###### Question A.\nL61: \nL62: Let $M$ be a closed aspherical manifold, such that its fundamental group $\\pi$ has trivial centre. When can a finite subgroup\nL63: \nL64: (1)  |  | $$G\\subset\\mathrm{Out}(\\pi)$$  |\nL65: \nL66: be refined to a $G$-action by homeomorphisms on $M$?\nL67: The triviality of the centre of $\\pi$ guarantees that $G\\subset\\mathrm{Out}(\\pi_{1}(M))$ refines to an $E_{1}$-action of $G$ on $M$, i.e. a map $BG\\rightarrow B\\mathrm{hAut}(M)$. See e.g. [cite37†Kre25 , p.3] for an elaboration. In particular, this allows us to speak of the homotopy fixed points $M^{hG}$ of the action. We give a purely group-theoretic description of this space below cite38†Eq. 2 .\nL68: Previous topological approaches to cite39†Question A , for example [cite40†DL24 ], relied on these homotopy fixed points to be equivalent to a finite set. This article gives the first purely topological construction of solutions to the Nielsen realisation problem in the case when $M^{hG}$ is low-dimensional compared to the dimension of $M$.\nL69: ###### Theorem B.\nL70: \nL71: Let $M^{d}$ be an oriented aspherical $d$-manifold with $\\pi=\\pi_{1}(M)$ word-hyperbolic and $d$ at least six. Consider a cyclic subgroup $C_{p}\\subset\\mathrm{Out}(\\pi)$ of odd prime order $p$. If the space $M^{hC_{p}}$ is homotopy equivalent to a PL-manifold of dimension at most $(d-2)/2$, then it refines to a $C_{p}$-action on $M$.\nL72: The generalised Nielsen realisation problem (cite39†Question A ) has attracted some interest in the literature. In [cite41†RS77 ], the importance of the triviality of the centre of $\\pi$ was first discovered, see [cite37†Kre25 ] for an elaboration. The article [cite42†BW08 ] gives counterexamples for $G$ being a cyclic group of order $2$. Lastly, there are some uniqueness and non-uniqueness results for solutions to the Nielsen realisation problem [cite43†CDK14 , cite44†CDK15 ].\nL73: In fact, the action of $C_{p}$ on $M$ we produce satisfies a special property: It is Borel in the sense that the map $M^{C_{p}}\\rightarrow M^{hC_{p}}$ is an equivalence. This design criterion is attractive for the following reason: Let $\\mathcal{O}\\coloneqq\\pi_{1}(M_{hC_{p}})$ denote the orbifold fundamental group of $(M,C_{p}\\subset\\mathrm{Out}(\\pi))$ (see cite11†Section 2 ).\nL74: That the action of $C_{p}$ on $M$ produced by cite45†Theorem B is Borel guarantees that $M$ is equivariantly homotopy equivalent to the quotient $\\pi\\backslash E_{\\mathrm{fin}}\\mathcal{O}$ with its residual $C_{p}$-action. Here, $E_{\\mathrm{fin}}\\mathcal{O}$ is the universal space for the family of finite subgroups of $\\mathcal{O}$.\nL75: Given a Borel $C_{p}$-action on $M$ as produced by cite45†Theorem B , the group $\\mathcal{O}$ acts on the universal cover $\\widetilde{M}$, exhibiting a cocompact manifold model for $E_{\\mathrm{fin}}\\mathcal{O}$.\nL76: The space $M^{hC_{p}}$ can be identified explicitly in terms of the group $\\mathcal{O}$. We show in cite46†Lemma A.4 that\nL77: \nL78: (2)  |  | $$M^{hC_{p}}\\simeq\\coprod_{F\\in\\mathcal{M}}BW_{\\mathcal{O}}F.$$  |\nL79: Here, $\\mathcal{M}$ is a set of representatives of the nontrivial finite subgroups of $\\mathcal{O}$ up to conjugation. The group $W_{\\mathcal{O}}F=N_{\\mathcal{O}}F/F$ is the Weyl group of $F$ in $\\mathcal{O}$. The condition in cite45†Theorem B can hence be read as a condition on the Weyl groups of finite subgroups of the orbifold fundamental group $\\mathcal{O}$.\nL80: For example, if the normalisers of nontrivial finite subgroups of $\\mathcal{O}$ are virtually cyclic, cite38†Eq. 2 identifies $M^{hC_{p}}$ as a disjoint union of circles and points. We also mention that the indexing set $\\mathcal{M}$ is a finite set, since a word-hyperbolic group has only finitely many conjugacy classes of finite subgroups [cite47†BH99 , Thm. III.$\\Gamma$.3.2.].\nL81: The formula cite38†Eq. 2 can also be used to see that it is in general not guaranteed for $M^{hC_{p}}$ to be homotopy equivalent to a closed manifold, see [cite48†FL04 ].\nL82: ### Outline of the proof of cite45†Theorem B L83: \nL84: The proof of cite45†Theorem B relies on equivariant and isovariant Poincaré duality, two tools designed to describe facets of the homotopy theory of manifolds with a group action. Write $X_{\\mathrm{Bor}}\\coloneqq\\pi\\backslash E_{\\mathrm{fin}}\\mathcal{O}$ (see cite11†Section 2 for an explanation of the notation).\nL85: \nL86:   1. (a)\nL87: Equivariant and isovariant Poincaré duality provide the structure of a finite semifree isovariant $C_{p}$-Poincaré space on $X_{\\mathrm{Bor}}$. That is, a decomposition of $X_{\\mathrm{Bor}}$ as a pushout of finite $C_{p}$-spaces\nL88: \nL89: (3)  |  |  |\nL90: satisfying some geometric properties. Among those properties is that the action on $C_{p}$ and $\\partial C_{p}$ is free, and that $(Q,\\partial Q)=C_{p}\\backslash(C,\\partial C)$ is a Poincaré duality pair. Here, the codimension assumption in cite45†Theorem B is used.\nL91: Intuitively cite49†Eq. 3 resembles the decomposition of a smooth $C_{p}$-manifold $M$ into its fixed point set $M^{C_{p}}$, the free part $M\\setminus M^{C_{p}}$ and that they glue to $M$ along the unit sphere bundle of the normal bundle of the fixed point set [cite50†KK25 , Constr. 1.2].\nL92:   2. (b)\nL93: \nL94: We use non-equivariant surgery theory for topological manifolds, in particular Ranicki’s total surgery obstruction to show that the pair $(Q,\\partial Q)$ is equivalent to a compact manifold pair $(N,\\partial N)$. The essential method here is a computation involving the $\\mathrm{K}$-and $\\mathrm{L}$-theoretic Farrell–Jones conjecture. This is one step where the hyperbolicity assumption on $\\pi$ is used.\nL95: \nL96:   3. (c)\nL97: Picking an equivalence $X_{\\mathrm{Bor}}^{C_{p}}\\simeq L$, where $L$ is a PL-manifold, we want to glue it to the $C_{p}$-cover $\\overline{N}$ of $N$ to obtain a manifold with $C_{p}$-action. For this, we use the technique of manifold approximate fibrations: the teardrop construction allows us to build a topology on the $C_{p}$-set $(\\overline{N}\\setminus\\partial\\overline{N})\\cup L$ giving a $C_{p}$-manifold $M^{\\prime}$.\nL98: As a last step, one shows that $M$ is homeomorphic to $M^{\\prime}$, compatible with the outer actions on the fundamental group.\nL99: Before moving on to how steps (a), (b) and (c) are carried out in this article specifically, we mention that also outside applications to the Nielsen realisation problem it presents a useful pipeline to construct $C_{p}$-actions on topological manifolds. First, one uses equivariant and isovariant Poincaré duality to separate the problem into the fixed point set part and the free part. Then one uses non-equivariant surgery to construct manifold structures on the free and fixed part separately.\nL100: Finally, the theory of approximate fibrations is used to glue fixed part and free part back together.\nL101: Step (a) relies on and motivated the equivariant Poincaré duality developed in [cite51†HKK24a , cite52†HKK24 ] and on the theory of their isovariant structures built in [cite50†KK25 ]. The work [cite53†Lüc ] is a precursor to these techniques, and heavily inspired them.\nL102: Step (b) is mostly computational, partially generalising and streamlining computations in [cite40†DL24 ] involving Farrell–Jones assembly maps. In contrast to [cite40†DL24 ] our presentation consequently uses the modern setup of hermitian $K$-theory developed in [cite54†CDH+23 , cite55†CDH+25 ], which might make the results therein accessible to a broader audience. We note some additional results made in the proof of this step below.\nL103: Step (c) is vastly simpler in the setting of discrete fixed points, than in our generality. Roughly, in the setting of discrete fixed points, the boundary $\\partial\\overline{N}$ will consist of spheres with a certain group action, and one can extend these group actions over a disc to obtain a group action on a closed manifold, which will in fact give the desired solution to the Nielsen realisation problem.\nL104: For high-dimensional fixed points, it is not true in general that the map $\\partial\\overline{N}\\rightarrow L$ is homotopic to an equivariant sphere bundle projection, so we cannot glue $\\overline{N}$ to $L$ by gluing in a disc bundle. So we use the technique of manifold approximate fibrations and the teardrop construction from [cite56†HTW+00 ] to deal with this gluing construction.\nL105: Since this technique is not broadly used, cite26†Section 5 might be of some independent interest for readers interested in constructing group actions on topological manifolds. We use the $\\mathrm{K}$-theoretic obstruction theory to deform maps into approximate fibrations from [cite57†FLS18 ]. As this obstruction theory is only developed over PL-manifolds, we need the PL-assumption in cite45†Theorem B .\nL106: ### 1.1. Additional results\nL107: \nL108: In showing the existence of a manifold structure on the complement quotient $(Q,\\partial Q)$ (i.e. the pair $C_{p}\\backslash(C,\\partial C)$ in cite49†Eq. 3 ) we prove some results on algebraic $K$-theory and Block homeomorphism groups that might be of independent interest. We start with the relevant $K$-theoretic results. Let $G$ be a finite group, and $\\pi$ a torsionfree group. Fix an extension\nL109: \nL110: (4)  |  | $$1\\rightarrow\\pi\\rightarrow\\Gamma\\rightarrow G\\rightarrow 1.$$  |\nL111: A subgroup $F\\subset\\Gamma$ is called full if the composite $F\\subset\\Gamma\\rightarrow G$ is an isomorphism. Write $\\mathcal{M}$ for a set of representatives of conjugacy classes of full subgroups. We assume that each nontrivial finite subgroup of $\\Gamma$ is contained in a unique full subgroup. See cite58†Lemma A.2 for alternative characterisations of this property.\nL112: The Whitehead group $\\mathrm{Wh}(\\Gamma)$ of $\\Gamma$ is relevant to the theory of $s$-cobordisms, and the reduced projective class group $\\mathrm{K}(\\mathbb{Z}\\Gamma)$ of $\\mathbb{Z}\\Gamma$ to Wall’s finiteness obstruction or Siebenmann’s end theorem.\nL113: ###### Theorem C.\nL114: \nL115: If $\\Gamma$ is a Farrell–Jones group as above, then the inclusions $N_{\\Gamma}F\\subset\\Gamma$ of normalisers of full subgroups of $\\Gamma$ induce isomorphisms\nL116: \nL117: (5)  |  | $$\\bigoplus_{F\\in\\mathcal{M}}\\mathrm{Wh}(N_{\\Gamma}F)\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}\\mathrm{Wh}(\\Gamma)\\hskip 8.53581pt\\text{and}\\hskip 8.53581pt\\bigoplus_{F\\in\\mathcal{M}}\\widetilde{\\mathrm{K}}_{0}(\\mathbb{Z}N_{\\Gamma}F)\\xrightarrow{\\simeq}\\widetilde{\\mathrm{K}}_{0}(\\mathbb{Z}\\Gamma).$$  |\nL118: Examples of Farrell–Jones groups are word-hyperbolic groups or fundamental groups of nonpositively curved manifolds, and finite extensions of such. Having this in mind, we give an application of our results to the study of block homeomorphism groups. Let $M$ be a nonpositively curved closed manifold with an isometric action by the finite group $G$ of odd order. Assume that the action is semifree and smooth, so only $G$ and the trivial group occur as isotropy groups.\nL119: We let $(\\overline{N},\\partial\\overline{N})$ denote the complement of an equivariant tubular neighborhood of $M^{G}$ in $M$, and set $(N,\\partial N)=G\\backslash(\\overline{N},\\partial\\overline{N})$. For these specific manifolds we get the following result for its group of block homeomorphisms.\nL120: ###### Theorem D.\nL121: \nL122: For $M$ as above, if $\\dim M\\geq 6$ and $M^{G}\\subset M$ has codimension at least $3$, the map\nL123: \nL124: (6)  |  | $$\\widetilde{\\mathrm{Homeo}}(N,\\partial N)\\rightarrow\\mathrm{hAut}(N,\\partial N)$$  |\nL125: \nL126: is an inclusion of path components onto the group of simple homotopy automorphisms.\nL127: The left hand side of cite59†Eq. 6 denotes the group of block homeomorphisms of $N$ respecting (but not fixing) the boundary, and the right hand side homotopy automorphisms of the pair $(N\\partial N)$. A homotopy automorphism $(f,\\partial f)\\colon(N,\\partial N)\\rightarrow(N,\\partial N)$ is simple if the maps $f$ and $\\partial f$ are simple homotopy equivalences. cite60†Theorem D resembles the conclusion of the Block Borel conjecture for block homeomorphisms of aspherical manifolds.\nL128: We view it as interesting, since most results for block homeomorphism groups of manifolds are known for manifolds with torsionfree fundamental group. See [cite61†HLL+21 ] for more information about the Block Borel conjecture, including its relevance to the vanishing results of characteristic classes.\nL129: ### 1.2. Recent literature on coherent actions\nL130: We mention some recent work on the theory of coherent actions on manifolds. If $M$ is a closed topological (resp. smooth) manifold and $G$ a compact Lie group, a coherent action on $M$ by homeomorphisms (resp. diffeomorphisms) is a map $\\alpha\\colon BG\\rightarrow B\\mathrm{Homeo}(M)$ (resp. $B\\mathrm{Diff}$). Following Reinhold, we call such an action kinetic if it comes from an actual (smooth) $G$-action on $M$.\nL131: Reinhold [cite62†Rei19 ] constructed non-kinetic smooth actions of the group $\\mathrm{SU}(2)$ on high-dimensional manifolds, and recently Krannich–Randal-Williams constructed some examples of non-kinetic coherent actions by finite groups [cite63†KR26 ]. The latter was inspired by a construction of Kang–Park–Taniguchi [cite64†KPT26 ], who also gave a similar construction in dimension four [cite65†KPT26a ].\nL132: In the results of this article, we start with a high-dimensional aspherical manifold $M$ with hyperbolic fundamental group and a map\nL133: (7)  |  | $$BC_{p}\\rightarrow B\\mathrm{hAut}(M)\\simeq B\\widetilde{\\mathrm{Homeo}}(M)$$  |\nL134: \nL135: and lift it to an actual group action under the conditions of cite45†Theorem B . It would be interesting to investigate the relation of the theory of coherent actions with the methods used in this article, including equivariant or isovariant Poincaré duality, and equivariant surgery.\nL136: ### 1.3. Conventions\nL137: We freely use the language of $\\infty$-categories as developed by Joyal and Lurie. The term category is used for an $\\infty$-category. We write $\\mathcal{S}$ for the category of spaces, and $\\mathrm{Sp}$ for the category of spectra. The term compact is used in its categorical meaning, so a compact space is a $G$-space of the homotopy type of a finitely dominated $G$-CW complex. The exception being that “compact manifold” is used for a manifold which is covering-compact.\nL138: We sometimes write $e$ for the trivial group, so if $X$ is a $G$-space the notation $X^{e}$ refers to fixed points of the trivial group, i.e. the underlying space. For a discrete group $\\Gamma$ and $F\\subset\\Gamma$ a subgroup, we write $N_{\\Gamma}F$ for its normaliser and $W_{\\Gamma}F=N_{\\Gamma}F/F$ for its Weyl group.\nL139: ### 1.4. Acknowledgements\nL140: I thank my advisor Wolfgang Lück for introducing me to the Nielsen realisation problem; this article is part of my PhD thesis. Further thanks go to Dominik Kirstein and Kaif Hilman for exploring equivariant Poincaré duality with me, which was crucial for this project. I thank Shmuel Weinberger for pointing out the relevance of approximate fibrations for equivariant manifold theory to me, and Andrea Bianchi, Emma Brink, Branko Juran, Markus Land as well as Marco Volpe for helpful conversations.\nL141: ###### Contents\nL142:   1. cite6†1 Introduction L143:     1. cite7†1.1 Additional results L144:     2. cite8†1.2 Recent literature on coherent actions L145:     3. cite9†1.3 Conventions L146:     4. cite10†1.4 Acknowledgements L147:   2. cite11†2 Setup and decompositions from equivariant Poincaré duality L148:     1. cite12†2.1 Good decompositions and isovariant Poincaré spaces L149:     2. cite13†2.2 Finiteness L150:     3. cite14†2.3 Existence of finite isovariant structures for $X_{\\mathrm{Bor}}$ --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://msp.org/gt/2000/4-1/p14.xhtml\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/exotic.pdf)\nciteturn6view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 34; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://people.math.osu.edu/davis.12/exotic.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1245\n"
